# Defect Dominated Hierarchical Ti-Metal-Organic Frameworks via a Linker Competitive Coordination Strategy for Toluene Removal

## abstract
Constructing a hierarchically porous structure in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can improve the accessibility of active sites and facilitate mass diffusion to enhance their adsorption and catalytic efficiency. Here, a novel linker competitive coordination strategy based on the electronegativity difference of two kinds of organic linkers is proposed to tailor the porous structure of Ti-MOF (MIL-125). A series of Ti-MOFs with continuously tunable hierarchical porosity is obtained by simply adjusting the molar ratios of two organic linkers. The demonstration of toluene removal shows that the competitive coordination strategy not only contributes to wide pore size distribution to enhance the adsorption performance of toluene, but also endows good charge separation ability to facilitate photocatalytic performance. Finally, the toluene removal efficiency of optimal Ti-MOF with mixed organic linkers of 1:1 molar ratio is 2.14 times and 1.88 times of the pristine MIL-125 and MIL-125(NH2), respectively. This strategy opens a new prospect to optimize Ti-MOF properties for various heterogeneous catalysis applications.

Constructing a hierarchically porous structure in metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) can improve the accessibility of active sites and facilitate mass diffusion to enhance their adsorption and catalytic efficiency. Here, a novel linker competitive coordination strategy based on the electronegativity difference of two kinds of organic linkers is proposed to tailor the porous structure of Ti-MOF (MIL-125). A series of Ti-MOFs with continuously tunable hierarchical porosity is obtained by simply adjusting the molar ratios of two organic linkers. The demonstration of toluene removal shows that the competitive coordination strategy not only contributes to wide pore size distribution to enhance the adsorption performance of toluene, but also endows good charge separation ability to facilitate photocatalytic performance. Finally, the toluene removal efficiency of optimal Ti-MOF with mixed organic linkers of 1:1 molar ratio is 2.14 times and 1.88 times of the pristine MIL-125 and MIL-125 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ , respectively. This strategy opens a new prospect to optimize Ti-MOF properties for various heterogeneous catalysis applications.
## 1. Introduction
As one of the typical volatile organic compounds (VOCs), toluene is released by many extensive sources, such as industry and automobile exhaust. It threatens the ecological environment and human health.[1] Recently, researchers observed that metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) can integrate gas adsorption and photocatalysis degradation for VOCs removal and exhibit great promising potentials.[2] MOFs, consisting of inorganic metal ions/clusters and organic linkers, possess a designable framework topology, high surface area, and tunable bandgap structure.[3] It has been regarded as some of the most compelling porous materials for many significant applications, such as gas adsorption, energy storage, and heterogeneous catalysis.[4] Nevertheless, their intrinsic microporous nature (pore size smaller than $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) profoundly restricts mass transfer kinetics and active site exposure.[5] Generally, the design of the framework topology structure or defect introduction has been suggested to be responsible for tuning the pore size distribution, resulting in enhanced performance for various applications.[6] In practice, several strategies, including solvent-assisted ligand exchange approach,[7] modulation of cation valence,[8] templating methods,[9] linker thermolysis,[6] and use of capping agents,[10] have been successfully exploited to construct MOFs with hierarchically porous structures. However, these methods usually result in limited pore size distribution, the introduction of impurities, or additional post-treatment processes, like template removal, acid etching, high-temperature annealing, and so on.[6,8–9,11] Therefore, the hierarchically porous MOFs with accurately customized pore size via a simple operation under relatively moderate conditions is highly desired.[12]
At present, most of the methods to control the formation of hierarchically porous MOF have focused on the crystal growth steps, topology structure design, or reaction conditions,[13] few approaches regarding the nucleation process have been adapted due to its random fluctuation and difficulty to predict.[14] Indeed, the nucleation process involves the coordination of organic linkers and metal clusters into subunits. The coordination ability is strongly dependent on the difference in electronegativity between organic linkers and metal ions/clusters.[15] [16] Based on the conjugation effect, the electronegativity of organic linkers could be regulated by modifying electron-withdrawing $(\cdot\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , and - ${\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ ) or electron-donating $\mathrm{\partial}[\cdot\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , -OH) groups on the benzene ring.[17] Thus, it is expected that mixing organic linkers with different electronegativity values can significantly affect the nucleation process due to the competitive electronic affinity.
In this study, a Ti-based metal-organic framework (MIL-125), a traditional microporous MOF built from a Ti-oxo cluster and 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ , was chosen for the proof of concept. In detail, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $\mathrm{(NH_{2}{\cdot}B D C^{2-}}$ : 3.2) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaBDC^{2-}}$ : 3.7) linkers with different electronegativities were employed to create competitive coordination in the nucleation stage.[16] As a result, the pore size distribution of MIL-125 is significantly modulated by adjusting the molar ratios of two kinds of organic linkers, achieving better adsorption and photocatalytic performance to remove toluene.
