## 2. Results and Discussion
As-synthesized Ti-MOFs with mixed linkers, denoted as Ti-MOF- $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}$ (x represents the molar ratio of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ to $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ were prepared by a one-pot hydrothermal method. The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface area and pore size distributions of Ti-MOFs were examined by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (Figure  1a,b) and density functional theory (DFT) model (Figure  1c,d). MIL-125 and MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ have normal micropore structures with dominant peaks located at 0.74, 0.80, and $0.72\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.[18] However, all Ti-MOF- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ possess a hierarchically porous structure because the isotherm of Ti-MOF-x consists of a sharp uptake $(P/P_{0}<0.1\$ , corresponding to type I) and a visible hysteresis loop $(P/P_{0}$ from 0.4 to 0.9, corresponding to type IV).[19] Based on the DFT pore size
The morphologies of a series of Ti-MOF samples were investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). As shown in Figure  2a the pristine MIL-125 synthesized with a single $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ linker shows a disk-like plate morphology with a (001) dominant crystal facet, while the MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ synthesized with a single $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ linker is composed of irregular truncated octahedrons (Figure  2b). A distinct morphology evolution process between pristine MIL-125 and MIL-125 $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ can be observed after mixing the linkers $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ (Figure $2\mathrm{a}{-}\mathrm{d}$ and Figure S2 (Supporting Information)). In particular, Ti-MOF-1 has tabular truncated tetragonal plates with two (001), four (110), and eight (111) crystal facets (Figure 2c).[22] Notably, unlike pristine MIL-125 and MIL-125 $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , which have smooth surfaces, all Ti-MOF-x structures present hierarchical pores, and the pore size gradually increases with decreasing x. The formation of hierarchical pores can be further confirmed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Compared to the TEM images of MIL-125 (Figure  2e) and MIL-125 $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ (Figure 2f), that of Ti-MOF-1 exhibits relatively bright spots that suggest the existence of hierarchical pores (Figure  2g). Moreover, these bright spots are more obvious and larger in the TEM image of Ti-MOF-0.25 (Figure 2h), which is consistent with the SEM observations (Figure 2d). Furthermore, the energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping of Ti-MOF-1 (Figure S3, Supporting Information) shows that this material consists of C, Ti, N, and O with the homogeneous distribution.
The electronic structures of MIL-125, MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , and Ti-MOF-1 were investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The high-resolution XPS spectra were transformed by Gaussian-Lorenzian analysis. The Ti 2p spectra of all the samples show two main peaks located at 458.8 and $464.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 3a), which are assigned to Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ , respectively.[23] Notably, the Ti 2p peak in the spectrum of Ti-MOF-1 shifts to lower binding energy than those of the other samples, suggesting that the generation of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ ions.[23–24] The O 1s peaks of Ti-MOF-1 that present three primary bonds of Ti-O, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and -OH at 530.1, 531.5 and $533.4\mathrm{eV}$ also shift to slightly lower binding energy, compared with those peaks of the other two samples (Figure 3b). On the other hand, as shown in Table S2 (Supporting Information), the linker content of Ti-MOF-1 calculated from the normalized peak area is lower than that of pristine MIL-125 and that of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as a result of the presence of the missing-linker defects. Likewise, the -OH content of Ti-MOF-1 shows an increment because the missing linker can be replaced by -OH.[25] The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of Ti-MOF-1 shows the lowest organic linker content, compared with MIL-125 and MIL-125 $(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , further confirming the existence of missing-linker defects (Figure  3c).[26] Correspondingly, to identify the kind of missing linker, the proton nuclear magnetic resonance $(^{1}\mathrm{H\NMR})$ spectra was performed (Figure  3d and Figure S4 (Supporting Information)).[27] Based on the integral value, the real content ratios of two kinds of organic linkers were calculated and summarized in Table S3 (Supporting Information).[28] Noticeably, all Ti-MOF-x present deviations of the content ratio of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}–\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{BDC^{2-}}$ from their theoretical values. Lower $\mathsf{B D C}^{2-}$ linker content of all TiMOF-x illustrates that the $\mathrm{BDC^{2-}}$ in missing-linker is dominated. Previous studies have reported that the missing-linker defects can lead to the construction of the hierarchically porous MOF.[20b] [29] Moreover, for MOFs structure, the missing-cluster defects also can contribute to forming the hierarchical pores.[21b, 30] To validate whether there are missing-cluster defects, inductively coupled plasm-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) measurements of the Ti-MOFs were measured and shown in Table S4 (Supporting Information). It is clearly observed that the Ti content of Ti-MOF-1 is much higher than that of MIL-125 and $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , which excludes the possibility of missingcluster defects, but confirms the missing-linker defects. Moreover, the molecular formulae for all samples were derived via combining the TGA curves (Figure S5, Supporting Information) and $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra. As shown in Table S5 (Supporting Information), the tendencies of the theoretical and real contents obtained by ICP-OES of the Ti content in as-prepared hierarchically porous Ti-MOF-x sample are similar. This result provides more solid evidence to demonstrate that the Ti content is only affected by missing-linker defects rather than missingcluster defects. Therefore, in this case, we can conclude that the missing-linker defects play a dominant role in creating the hierarchically porous Ti-MOF.
According to the particle size distribution (Figures S6 and S7, Supporting Information), the size order of these samples is $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125<Ti-MOF- $4<$ Ti-MOF-2<Ti-MOF- $\cdot1<$ Ti-MOF$0.5{<}\mathrm{Ti}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOF}{-}0.25{<}\mathrm{MIL}{-}125$ , indicating that the particle size gradually increases with the increasing molar ratio of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ . Generally, the nuclear concentration gradients of MOFs determine the growth step and rate, hence enabling different size distributions. The $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group can provide more electrons to increase the deprotonation rate of carboxyl groups in organic linkers, and the stronger coordination ability of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ accelerates the nucleation process, resulting in the formation of smaller particles.[31] Meanwhile, according to the photographs of the MIL-125, MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , and Ti-MOF-1 precursor solutions, yellow precipitation can be produced with the presence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ linker during the stirring process(Figure S8, Supporting Information), which also suggests the presence of competitive coordination. Therefore, the formation of missinglinker defects might be caused by the competitive coordination at the nucleation stage.
In order to explore the generation mechanism of missinglinker defects, the turbid solution of Ti-MOF-1 precursor was pre-treated by ultrasonication (the obtained sample is Ti-MOF1S) because short-time ultrasonication treatment can increase the nucleation rate to minimize the influence of linker coordination competition.[32] As expected, the Ti-MOF-1S only presents a microporous structure, although it has mixed linkers (Figure S9a-d, Supporting Information). The XRD analysis demonstrates that the crystal structure of Ti-MOF-1S is consistent with pristine MIL-125 (Figure S9e, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the TGA (Figure S9f, Supporting Information), XPS (Figure S10 and Table S2, Supporting Information), $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR (Figure S11a and Table S3, Supporting Information), and ICP-OES (Table S4, Supporting Information) of the Ti-MOF-1S can identify that the missing of $\mathrm{BDC^{2-}}$ is remarkably suppressed by the short-time ultrasonication treatment. These analysis results point to that competitive coordination is the primary governing factor in the construction of a hierarchically porous structure.
To deeper understand the formation of the missing-linker defects, the precipitation of Ti-MOF-1 before the ultrasonic treatment, denoted as Ti-MOF-1 precursor, was analyzed. The SEM image (Figure S12, Supporting Information) of the Ti-MOF-1 precursor discloses that it consists of nanosized primary particles that can act as the building units to construct the MOF.[33] As shown in Figure S11b and Table S3 (Supporting Information), the content ratio of organic linkers in the Ti-MOF-1 precursor suggests that a certain amount of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ was coordinated with the $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ cluster during the stirring process because the intermolecular hydrogen bond between the function group of the ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ and -COOH in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ can retard further nucleation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}^{[28]}$ and induce more $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ for coordination. It is worth notice that the content ratio of $\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ in the Ti-MOF-1 precursor is close to that in the Ti-MOF-1-S and much higher than that in Ti-MOF-1. However, the uncoordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ should be normally dissolved in the solvent and removed by centrifugation, so the content ratios of $\mathrm{BDC^{2-}}$ in Ti-MOF-1 precursor should be lower than that of Ti-MOF-1S. Considering the sonication treatment can promote the desorption of adsorbed substance, we can conclude that part of uncoordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ might be adsorbed in the cavity of the Ti-MOF-1 precursor rather than coordinate with the Ti-O cluster.
According to the above results, a possible formation mechanism of hierarchical pores is proposed. As shown in Scheme 1 the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{BDC}}^{2-}$ with lower electronegativity can first coordinate with the $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ ion, resulting in a higher electron density cloud of the $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ ion. Then, part of $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ can be induced to coordinate with these unsaturated $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ ions to form the secondary building units (SBUs) with mixed organic linkers due to the present hydrogen bond between - $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and -COOH in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}B D C}$ .[28] Subsequently, in the presence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ , the formed SBUs can be rapidly expanded to primary particles, resulting in a turbid solution.[34] At the same time, part of unreacted $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ can be adsorbed in the cavity of these nanosized primary particles and restricted to joining the secondary nucleation. Thereby, some missing-linker defects can be gradually formed in these nanosized primary particles. Finally, the hierarchically porous Ti-MOF is constructed by the self-assembly of primary particles during the growth stage.
UV-vis absorption spectra of these synthesized Ti-MOFs are shown in Figure S13a (Supporting Information). The MIL-125 has an absorption edge near $330\ \mathrm{nm}$ , whereas the absorption edge of MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ is shifted to $530~\mathrm{nm}$ because the amine functional group $\left(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , as a typical chromophore, can provide more conjugated $\pi$ electrons to Ti-oxo clusters.[17] Correspondingly, the presence of $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in all Ti-MOF- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ samples can also contribute to the observation that the optical absorption is similar to that of MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ , which is consistent with the previous report.[35] The bandgaps were further estimated by Tauc plot analysis. The bandgaps of Ti-MOF-x are between those of MIL-125 and MIL-125 $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ (Figure S13b and Table S6, Supporting Information). The reduced bandgap of Ti-MOF- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ is because the electron can transfer from the aromatic amines to the Ti-oxo cluster through conjugated $\pi$ electron transition.[17,35]
## 3. Conclusion
In this work, we have demonstrated the regulation of the hierarchical porosity of Ti-MOFs by using a linker competitive coordination strategy. The hierarchically porous structure is constructed by the missing-linker defects generated by the adsorption of unpaired $\mathrm{BDC^{2-}}$ in the cavity of nanosized MOF particles. The pore size distribution of the obtained Ti-MOF- $\mathbf{\nabla\cdotX}$ was continuously tuned by judicious adjusting the molar ratio between the $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}B D C}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ . Meanwhile, the bandgap structure was optimized by the organic linker with chromophores $\left(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ . The enhanced performance of Ti-MOF-1 was confirmed in terms of both adsorption performance and charge separation efficiency for photocatalytic toluene degradation. This work discovers a simple but efficient strategy to multidimensionally optimize the pore structure and solar energy conversion efficiency of Ti-MOF for comprehensive applications.
## 4. Experimental Section
All chemical reagents were analytical grade and used without further purification. DMF $(99.5\%)$ and methanol $(99.8\%)$ were provided by Samchun Chemicals. Terephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ , 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(N H_{2}\cdot H_{2}B D C)$ and titanium isopropoxide were purchased from Aldrich.
Ti-MOFs Preparation: A series of Ti-MOFs were prepared by a one-pot solvothermal method. Ti-MOF-1 was chosen as an illustration object to demonstrate the experimental process. Typically, 3 mmol of $N H_{2}\cdot H_{2}B D C$ and 3  mmol of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ were thoroughly mixed in $20~\mathsf{m L}$ of pure DMF. After complete dissolution, $2.2~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol was added to the above solution. One hour later, $0.9~\upmu\upiota$ of titanium isopropoxide was injected into the uniform mixed solution and continue stirring until precipitating occurred. The obtained suspension was then transferred into a $60~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After being cooled naturally to room temperature, the obtained precipitation was washed with DMF and methanol separately three times to remove the residual organic ligands and metal salts. The harvested precipitate was collected by centrifugation. The obtained powder was vacuum dried at $\mathsf{l}00^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ . To prepare a series of Ti-MOFs with different pore sizes, the molar ratios of $H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ was adjusted to 1:0, 4:1, 2:1, 1:1, 1:2, 1:4, and 0:1 (The total molar amount of organic linker is 6 mmol). The corresponding samples were denoted as MIL-125 $(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ , Ti-MOF-4, Ti-MOF-2, Ti-MOF-1, Ti-MOF-0.5, Ti-MOF-0.25, and MIL-125, respectively. The precursor of sonication sample (Ti-MOF-1S) was subjected to an additional ultrasonication treatment (Maximum Power, Powersonic 420, Korea) for $\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{10~s}}}$ after $20\mathrm{\min}$ of precipitation formation, and then keeps on stirring for another 10 min. (A longer sonication time will destroy the Ti-MOF crystallinity and result in an amorphous structure.) All other conditions were the same as those used for Ti-MOF-1.
Characterization: The morphologies and pore structures of the as-prepared Ti-MOFs were measured by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, JSM-7000F, Japan). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) measurements were performed by using a JEOL JEM-2100F (Japan) electron microscope. The crystal structure of Ti-MOF was determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using a Siemens D500/5000 diffractometer in Bragg-Brentano geometry under $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was obtained on a SESXPS instrument (ESCA2000, VG Microtech, England). $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were carried out on a Autosorb-iQ 2ST/MP equipment (Quantachrome) at a temperature of $77~\mathsf{K}.$ TGA was conducted on TA Instruments $Q50$ equipment under a $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ atmosphere $(20~\mathsf{m L}~\mathsf{m i n}^{-1})$ at a heating rate of $70^{\circ}\mathsf C\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ within the $30-750~^{\circ}C$ temperature range. The synthetic sample was dry at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $2\ h$ before TGA and NMR analysis. The liquid $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectroscopy was recorded with an AVANCE III HD 400 spectrometer (400 HZ). The analytical solution was prepared by digesting $5~\mathsf{m g}$ dry samples in $\rceil\mathrm{~\textsf~{~M~}~N a O H/D_{2}O}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . ICP-OES was carried out by an inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (OPTIMA 8300). UV-vis absorption spectra were obtained using a UV-vis spectrophotometer (Shimadzu UV-2550a).
Photoelectrochemical Measurements: Transient photocurrent response measurements and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were performed by means of a potentiostat (Chenhua Instrument,
CHI 660) using a standard three-electrode system with an ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ reference electrode (in $3\mathsf{\Gamma}\mathsf{M\Gamma}\mathsf{K C l}_{\iota}^{\prime}$ ) and a Pt foil counter electrode under a solar simulator (Peccell, Yokohama, Japan, PECL01) with $100\ m\times10^{-2}$ . A $0.5~\mathsf{m}$ ${\mathsf N}{\mathsf a}_{2}{\mathsf S}{\mathsf O}_{4}$ solution was employed as the electrolyte. All working electrodes were prepared as follows: $5.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of as-synthesized Ti-MOFs was added to $7m L$ of DMF and sonicated to develop a homogeneous suspension. Subsequently, the $200~\upmu\upiota$ of as-obtained suspension was doped onto a $2c m\times1$ cm F-doped $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ -coated glass (FTO glass) electrode to develop a uniform electrode. After the electrode dried naturally, it was vacuum dried at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\textmd{h}$ to ensure that the DMF was completely volatile. The transient photocurrent response of the samples was measured under chopped light at $0.5~\mathsf{V}.$ The EIS testing was performed with a bias potential of $\cdot0.3\mathrm{~V~}$ with a frequency range from $\overline{{10^{-1}}}$ to ${\mathsf{7}}0^{6}{\mathsf{H z}}$ .
Reactor Setup and Toluene Removal Procedure: As a representative VOCs pollutant, toluene was selected as the model pollutant to evaluate the adsorption-photodegradation performance of the as-synthesized Ti-MOFs. This experiment was carried out in a home-designed circulation reactor $(7m^{3})$ with a quartz window and magnetic stirrer under ambient conditions. In addition, this reactor was connected to a photoacoustic gas monitor (INNOVA 1412i, LumaSense Technologies) by a Teflon tube $(4~\mathsf{m m}$ diameter, $\textsf{l m}$ length), which can provide realtime monitoring of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration value.
The obtained Ti-MOFs were coated on a glass substrate, and the preparation steps were as follows: $_{0.2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of Ti-MOF powder was ground in $1m L$ of water for a few minutes. Then, the as-obtained slurry was dropped onto a clean glass substrate $(2~{\mathsf{c m}}\times4~{\mathsf{c m}})$ and dried at room temperature. After that, it was placed inside the reactor, and the surface covered with the sample faced the quartz window. The reactor was purged by high-purity nitrogen and oxygen until the baseline became stable. Next, ${\mathsf{1}}.0\ {\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of toluene was injected into the reactor, corresponding to an initial concentration of 230 ppm. After the adsorption process, a photocatalytic test was conducted under a solar simulator (Peccell, Yokohama, Japan, PECL01) with $100\ m\times10^{-2}$ . The photodegradation removal efficiency of Ti-MOFs was calculated by using the following formula (unit: ppm):
